.TH BORINGCA 1 "October 2026" "boringca 5.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
boringca \- quickly create a CA and issue child certificates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boringca
.br
.B boringca
.I name
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B init
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B issue
.I name
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B install\-trust
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B uninstall\-trust
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B boringca
.B help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boringca
is a small, self-contained command-line tool to create a root
Certificate Authority and issue server/client certificates signed by
it, for local development, home lab and internal PKI use. All
certificate generation and signing happens in-process (rcgen, ring
backend); no external openssl binary or other tool is invoked at
runtime. Keys are ECDSA P-256. It is NOT a replacement for a
production CA/PKI system.
.PP
Run with no arguments, \fBboringca\fR sets up the CA on first use and
prints where it is stored. Run as \fBboringca\fR \fIname\fR, it issues a
certificate for \fIname\fR (a DNS name or an IP address) signed by that
CA, creating the CA first if it doesn't exist yet -- this is shorthand
for \fBboringca issue\fR \fIname\fR, and accepts the same options. The
\fBinit\fR and \fBissue\fR subcommands below give full control over every
option. Options may also be given as \fB\-\-option=value\fR, and
\fB\-h\fR/\fB\-\-help\fR is accepted after any subcommand.
\fBinstall\-trust\fR adds the CA to the system trust store, and
\fBuninstall\-trust\fR removes it again; see
.B TRUSTING THE CA
below.
.SH INIT OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-cn " name"
Common Name of the root CA (default: "BoringCA Root").
.TP
.BI \-\-days " n"
Validity in days (default: 3650).
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
CA store directory (default: \fB$BORINGCA_HOME\fR or \fI~/.boringca\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an existing CA in \fB\-\-dir\fR.
.SH ISSUE OPTIONS
.TP
.I name
Short name for the certificate, used to derive file names
(\fIprivate/name.key\fR, \fIcerts/name.crt\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-cn " name"
Common Name (default: the certificate's \fIname\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-san " list"
Comma-separated Subject Alternative Names, e.g.
\fIdns:example.com,dns:www.example.com,ip:10.0.0.1\fR
(types: \fIdns\fR, \fIip\fR, \fIemail\fR, \fIuri\fR). Default:
\fIip:<cn>\fR if the CN is an IP address, \fIdns:<cn>\fR if it is a host
name; a CN that is neither (e.g. \fIuser@laptop\fR) is an error for a
server certificate, and gives no SAN for a \fB\-\-client\fR one.
.TP
.B \-\-server
Issue a server certificate, \fIextendedKeyUsage=serverAuth\fR (default).
.TP
.B \-\-client
Issue a client certificate, \fIextendedKeyUsage=clientAuth\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-both
Issue a certificate valid for both server and client authentication.
\fB\-\-server\fR, \fB\-\-client\fR and \fB\-\-both\fR are mutually
exclusive.
.TP
.BI \-\-days " n"
Validity in days (default: 825), capped to the CA's own expiry date.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
CA store directory (default: \fB$BORINGCA_HOME\fR or \fI~/.boringca\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace an existing certificate with the same \fIname\fR (without it,
issuing an existing name fails).
.SH INSTALL\-TRUST AND UNINSTALL\-TRUST OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " path"
CA store directory (default: \fB$BORINGCA_HOME\fR or \fI~/.boringca\fR).
.SH FILES
Under the CA store directory:
.RS
.TP
.I ca.key, ca.crt
Root CA private key and certificate.
.TP
.I ca.cn
The CA's Common Name. Informational only: the Common Name is read from
.I ca.crt
when signing.
.TP
.I private/<name>.key
Issued certificate's private key.
.TP
.I certs/<name>.crt
Issued certificate.
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORINGCA_HOME
Default CA store directory, used when \fB\-\-dir\fR is not given.
.SH TRUSTING THE CA
Run
.B boringca install\-trust
to add the CA to the system trust store. It detects which mechanism is
present from the distribution's trust anchors directory (
.BR update\-ca\-certificates (8)
on Debian/Ubuntu and openSUSE,
.BR update\-ca\-trust (8)
on Fedora/RHEL, or
.BR trust (1)
on Arch; the tools are also looked up in the sbin directories) and runs
the required steps through
.BR sudo (8)
(or
.BR doas (1)
when there is no sudo, or directly when already root and neither is
installed),
prompting for a password as needed -- this never happens implicitly (e.g.
merely because boringca itself is run as root); it is only ever done when
\fBinstall\-trust\fR is invoked explicitly. The certificate file installed
under
.IR /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ ,
.I /etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors/
or
.I /etc/pki/trust/anchors/
is named after the store directory plus a short hash of its absolute
path, so certificates from different \fB\-\-dir\fR stores never collide,
even when their directories share the same name.
.PP
If no known mechanism is found, the system-wide step fails with an error
instead of guessing; place
.I ca.crt
wherever locally-trusted CAs are expected on your system, or import it
into your browser/OS trust store directly.
.PP
\fBinstall\-trust\fR also tries, best-effort, to trust the CA in Firefox
and Chromium-based browsers. On Linux these never consult the system
trust store above -- each keeps its own NSS certificate database instead
(a
.I cert9.db
per Firefox profile, found via
.I profiles.ini
of the classic, Snap or Flatpak install, and one shared by
Chromium/Chrome under
.IR ~/.pki/nssdb ,
plus the Chromium Snap's own).
This step needs
.BR certutil (1)
(Debian/Ubuntu package
.BR libnss3-tools )
and runs entirely as the invoking user, with no
.BR sudo (8)
involved. A failure here (missing
.BR certutil ,
no Firefox profile, ...) is reported per browser and does not fail the
command -- restart the browser afterwards for it to notice the new CA.
.PP
.B boringca uninstall\-trust
undoes all of the above for the store given by \fB\-\-dir\fR: it removes
the CA from the system trust store (through
.BR sudo (8),
then refreshing the system bundle) and from the same browser databases,
and leaves the CA itself in its directory -- delete that directory
afterwards if it is no longer needed. Entries are found by the store's
unique name, so this still works after the store directory has been
deleted (except on Arch, where
.BR trust (1)
needs
.I ca.crt
to find the anchor); entries of other stores are never touched.
.SH SHELL COMPLETION
A bash completion script is shipped in
.IR completions/boringca.bash .
The Debian package installs it automatically (via
.BR debhelper (7)'s
.BR dh_bash-completion (1),
see
.IR debian/boringca.bash\-completion ),
so
.B TAB
completion of subcommands and their options works out of the box after
installing the
.B .deb
package -- nothing to run or source by hand. Outside of that package,
copy or symlink the file into
.I /usr/share/bash-completion/completions/boringca
yourself, or source it from your shell startup file. Only bash is
currently supported.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
boringca init --cn "Home Lab CA"
boringca issue nas --san dns:nas.lan,ip:192.168.1.10
boringca issue laptop --client --cn "fabrice@laptop"
boringca install-trust
boringca uninstall-trust
.EE
